{"element":{"atomicNumber":63,"symbol":"Eu","name":"Europium","wikidataId":"Q1396","wikipediaTitle":"Europium","wikipediaUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europium","period":6,"group":null,"block":"f","category":"lanthanide","phase":"solid","mass":151.964,"electronegativity":null,"ionizationEnergy":5.67,"radius":233,"summary":"Europium is a chemical element; it has symbol Eu and atomic number 63. It is a silvery-white metal of the lanthanide series that reacts readily with air to form a dark oxide coating. Europium is the most chemically reactive, least dense, and softest of the lanthanides. It is soft enough to be cut with a knife. Europium was discovered in 1896, provisionally designated as Σ; in 1901, it was named after the continent of Europe. Europium usually assumes the oxidation state +3, like other members of the lanthanide series, but compounds having oxidation state +2 are also common. All europium compounds with oxidation state +2 are slightly reducing. Europium has no significant biological role but is relatively non-toxic compared to other heavy metals. Most applications of europium exploit the phosphorescence of europium compounds. Europium is one of the rarest of the rare-earth elements on Earth.","neighbors":["Sm","Gd"],"rankings":{"mass":56,"electronegativity":0,"ionizationEnergy":86,"radius":25,"density":62,"meltingPoint":58,"boilingPoint":60,"halfLife":0},"sources":{"structured":{"provider":"PubChem","license":"public domain","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/element/63"},"identifiers":{"provider":"Wikidata","license":"CC0 1.0","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1396"},"summary":{"provider":"Wikipedia","title":"Europium","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europium","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","accessDate":"2026-03-30"}},"discoveryYear":1901,"discoverer":"Eugène-Anatole Demarçay","etymologyOrigin":"place","etymologyDescription":"Europe","density":5.24,"meltingPoint":1095,"boilingPoint":1802,"halfLife":null},"group":null,"anomalies":[{"slu